THIRD-PARTY COMPONENTS

1. Go modules compiled into the released binaries and container images

   This list is kept honest by an architecture test, and it asks the linker
   rather than go.mod: a module can be required and never end up in anything
   we publish. `go version -m lasthelp` prints the same inventory out of a
   binary you already have.

   SQLite driver, pure Go — no cgo, which is why the binary is a single static
   file:

     modernc.org/sqlite                  BSD 3-Clause
     modernc.org/libc                    BSD 3-Clause
     modernc.org/mathutil                BSD 3-Clause
     modernc.org/memory                  BSD 3-Clause
     github.com/remyoudompheng/bigfft    BSD 3-Clause
     github.com/google/uuid              BSD 3-Clause
     golang.org/x/sys                    BSD 3-Clause

   Sizes and durations written the way a person reads them:

     github.com/dustin/go-humanize       MIT

   Password hashing (argon2id):

     golang.org/x/crypto                 BSD 3-Clause

   Everything else this binary does uses the Go standard library.

   Required by go.mod and NOT in what we publish. The SQLite driver lists them
   for platforms this project does not build for, and the linker leaves them
   out of the Linux binaries and images, which are the only artefacts released.
   Named here because the requirement is real even though the code is absent:

     github.com/mattn/go-isatty          MIT
     github.com/ncruces/go-strftime      BSD 3-Clause

2. Build-time only, not redistributed

   TypeScript (Apache-2.0) and bun's bundler build the frontend. Neither ends
   up in the binary.
